## The Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad

### Peace Invocation (*Śāntiḥ Pāṭha*)

**Devanāgarī**
ॐ भद्रं कर्णेभिः शृणुयाम देवाः ।
भद्रं पश्येमाक्षभिर्यजत्राः ।
स्थिरैरङ्गैस्तुष्टुवागंसस्तनूभिः ।
व्यशेम देवहितं यदायूः ।
ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥

**IAST**
oṃ bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ śṛṇuyāma devāḥ |
bhadraṃ paśyemākṣabhir yajatrāḥ |
sthirair aṅgais tuṣṭuvāgaṃsas tanūbhiḥ |
vyaśema devahitaṃ yad āyūḥ |
oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||

**Pratibhā Translation**
O gods, may we hear only the auspicious with our ears. May we see only the auspicious with our eyes. With steady limbs and bodies, may we accomplish the life the gods have ordained for us. Om — peace, peace, peace.

**Commentary**
The triple *śāntiḥ* addresses the three sources of potential disruption: *ādhibhautika* (arising from external beings), *ādhidaivika* (arising from cosmic forces), and *ādhyātmika* (arising from within oneself). The prayer does not ask for the removal of difficulty but for the perceptual and constitutional steadiness to meet it. This is the precise disposition the Upaniṣad will ask of the listener.

