## Verse 2

### Devanāgarī
सर्वं ह्येतद् ब्रह्म अयमात्मा ब्रह्म सोऽयमात्मा चतुष्पात् ॥ २ ॥

### IAST
sarvaṃ hy etad brahma ayam ātmā brahma so 'yam ātmā catuṣpāt || 2 ||

### Pratibhā Translation
All this is indeed Brahman. This Ātman is Brahman. This Ātman has four quarters.

### Pratibhā Commentary
Three sentences that could each be the sole content of a philosophical tradition. *Sarvaṃ hy etad brahma* — all this is indeed Brahman — is a declaration of non-separation: the apparent multiplicity of the world is not other than the one undivided ground. *Ayam ātmā brahma* — this Ātman is Brahman — is the great identity statement, the *mahāvākya* that grounds Advaita: the individual principle of self-awareness and the universal ground of being are identical. Not similar. Not derivative. Identical.

Then: *so 'yam ātmā catuṣpāt* — this Ātman has four quarters. The apparent tension between the absolute declaration of unity and the analytical division into four is deliberate. The four-quarter analysis is not a description of how the Self actually is — it is a pedagogical instrument. Like a coin divided into four quarters: the coin is one, but the quarters serve to make it exchangeable, usable, comprehensible. Śaṅkara is explicit: the four quarters are *imagined* in Ātman to facilitate understanding. The point of the entire analysis that follows is to dissolve itself — to show that when the first three quarters are fully seen through, the fourth (Turīya) reveals itself as what was always the case.

### Key Terms
- **brahma** — the absolute ground of being; from *bṛh* (to expand, to be great); that which is greatest and expands infinitely
- **ātmā** — the Self; reflexive principle of self-awareness; here equated with Brahman, dissolving the distinction between individual and universal
- **catuṣpāt** — four-footed, four-quartered; *catuṣ* (four) + *pāt* (foot/quarter); the fourfold analytical schema

### Kārikā Resonances · *Āgama Prakaraṇa* I.10–12

**I.10**: निवृत्तेः सर्वदुःखानामीशानः प्रभुरव्ययः । अद्वैतः सर्वभावानां देवस्तुर्यो विभुः स्मृतः ॥

*nivṛtteḥ sarvaduḥkhānām īśānaḥ prabhur avyayaḥ | advaitaḥ sarvabhāvānāṃ devas turyo vibhuḥ smṛtaḥ ||*

The All-pervading is traditionally known as Turya — capable of controlling the cessation of all miseries, powerful, immutable, non-dual among all entities, refulgent.

**I.11**: कार्यकारणबद्धौ ताविष्येते विश्वतैजसौ । प्राज्ञः कारणबद्धस्तु द्वौ तौ तुर्ये न सिध्यतः ॥

*kāryakāraṇabaddhau tāv iṣyete viśvataijasau | prājñaḥ kāraṇabaddhas tu dvau tau turye na sidhyataḥ ||*

Viśva and Taijasa are conditioned by cause and effect; Prājña is conditioned by cause alone. Those two — cause and effect — have no locus standi in the case of Turya.

**Commentary**: This is the ontological key. The first three states are conditioned — Viśva by both cause and effect (gross causality), Taijasa by cause and effect at the subtle level, Prājña by cause alone (it is the causal state from which the others emerge). Turīya alone is unconditioned — it is neither cause nor effect. This means liberation is not something that *results* from anything. Turīya was never in the causal chain. It cannot be achieved because achievement is causal. It can only be recognized.

**I.12**: नात्मानं न परांश्चैव न सत्यं नापि चानृतम् । प्राज्ञः किंचन संवेत्ति तुर्यं तत्सर्वदृक्सदा ॥

*nātmānaṃ na parāṃś caiva na satyaṃ nāpi cānṛtam | prājñaḥ kiṃcana saṃvetti turyaṃ tat sarvadṛk sadā ||*

Neither the Self, nor others, for the matter of that; neither truth, nor again the untruth — nothing whatever does Prājña comprehend. That Turya however is always all-seeing.

### Cross-Tradition Resonance
- Aṣṭāvakra Gītā 2.1: *aho nirañjanaḥ śānto bodho'haṃ prakṛteḥ paraḥ* — Janaka's recognition "I am the stainless, peaceful knowing beyond nature" is the lived form of *ayam ātmā brahma*. The Māṇḍūkya states it; the Aṣṭāvakra shows it being recognized.
- Pratyabhijñāhṛdayam 13: *tatparijñāne cittam evāntarmukhī-bhāvena cetanāpadādhyārohāc citiḥ* — the mind itself, through introversion, becomes Citi. The Pratyabhijñā frames this as ascent; the Māṇḍūkya as recognition of what was always the case.
- Meister Eckhart, Sermon 52: "The ground of God and the ground of the soul are one ground." The closest Western parallel to *ayam ātmā brahma* — individual ground and absolute ground as not two.

### Practice (*Abhyāsa*)
Before any experience arises — before thought, before sensation — there is the simple fact of being aware. This bare awareness, which you cannot step outside of and examine as an object, is *ātmā*. The text says this is *brahma* — the ground of all existence. Not similar to it. Not a part of it. Identical. Sit with "this is that" for five minutes. Not as a belief to maintain but as a recognition to allow.

