## Verse 5 · *Suṣupti — Deep Sleep*

### Devanāgarī
यत्र सुप्तो न कञ्चन कामं कामयते न कञ्चन स्वप्नं पश्यति तत्सुषुप्तम् । सुषुप्तस्थान एकीभूतः प्रज्ञानघन एवानन्दमयो ह्यानन्दभुक् चेतोमुखः प्राज्ञस्तृतीयः पादः ॥ ५ ॥

### IAST
yatra supto na kañcana kāmaṃ kāmayate na kañcana svapnaṃ paśyati tat suṣuptam | suṣuptasthāna ekībhūtaḥ prajñānaghana evānandamayo hy ānandabhuk cetomukhaḥ prājñas tṛtīyaḥ pādaḥ || 5 ||

### Pratibhā Translation
That is deep sleep wherein the sleeper desires no desire and sees no dream. The third quarter is Prājña — whose sphere is deep sleep, in whom all experience is unified, who is verily a mass of consciousness, full of bliss, who experiences bliss, and who is the door to the knowing of the other two states.

### Pratibhā Commentary
The verse begins by defining deep sleep negatively — through absence of desire and dream — then immediately pivots to positive characterization. This is methodologically important: the text refuses to let deep sleep be merely an absence. The four positive descriptions are carefully chosen:

*Ekībhūtaḥ* — "become one" — names the unification of experience. The multiplicity of waking and dreaming collapses into undifferentiated unity. This is not the unity of recognition but the unity of non-differentiation — which is why it is not liberation.

*Prajñānaghana* — "mass of consciousness" — *ghana* means dense, massive, consolidated. Consciousness in deep sleep is not dispersed across objects but concentrated in undivided mass. The image is of space that becomes apparent *density* when no objects divide it — the space itself hasn't changed but the differentiation has ceased.

*Ānandamaya* — "full of bliss" — not the bliss of pleasant experience (which requires subject-object duality) but the inherent ease of consciousness not fragmented by desire and object. This is why sleep is restorative: in returning to the causal state, something of one's original nature is recovered.

*Cetomukha* — "having consciousness as its mouth/door" — Prājña is the gateway through which the other two states emerge and into which they return. The causal state contains the other states in seed form (*bīja*). This is also why Prājña is the doorway to understanding: by examining what is present in deep sleep (consciousness without objects) and absent (desire, duality), one can begin to sense what Turīya is.

### Key Terms
- **suṣupti** — deep sleep; from *su* (well) + *supti* (sleeping); fully, properly asleep
- **prājña** — the knowing one; the third-quarter experiencer; from *prajñā* (comprehensive knowing); paradoxically named "the knower" in the state where ordinary knowing is absent
- **ekībhūta** — become one, unified; the collapse of multiplicity into undifferentiated unity
- **prajñānaghana** — mass of consciousness; the density metaphor for unified, objectless awareness
- **ānandamaya** — made of bliss, full of bliss; the inherent ease of undivided consciousness
- **cetomukha** — having consciousness as its door; Prājña as the causal gateway

### Kārikā Resonances · *Āgama Prakaraṇa* I.13–16

**I.13**: द्वैतस्याग्रहणं तुल्यं प्राज्ञतुर्ययोः । बीजनिद्रायुतः प्राज्ञः सा च तुर्ये न विद्यते ॥

*dvaitasyāgrahaṇaṃ tulyaṃ prājñaturyayoḥ | bījanidrayutaḥ prājñaḥ sā ca turye na vidyate ||*

The non-perception of duality is common to both Prājña and Turya. Prājña is stuck up with the causal sleep, while it does not exist in Turya.

**I.14**: स्वप्ननिद्रायुतावाद्यौ प्राज्ञस्त्वस्वप्ननिद्रया । न निद्रां नैव च स्वप्नं तुर्ये पश्यन्ति निश्चिताः ॥

*svapnanidrayutāv ādyau prājñas tv asvapnanidrayā | na nidrāṃ naiva ca svapnaṃ turye paśyanti niścitāḥ ||*

The first two are stuck up with dream and sleep; Prājña, on the other hand, with dreamless sleep. The convinced ones [about Advaita] do see in Turya neither sleep nor dream for the matter of that.

**I.15**: अन्यथा गृह्णतः स्वप्नो निद्रा तत्त्वमजानतः । विपर्यासे तयोः क्षीणे तुरीयं पदमश्नुते ॥

*anyathā gṛhṇataḥ svapno nidrā tattvam ajānataḥ | viparyāse tayoḥ kṣīṇe turīyaṃ padam aśnute ||*

Dream is for one comprehending reality otherwise; sleep for one who does not know reality. When the wrong apprehension in those two becomes extinct, one attains to the Fourth stage.

**I.16**: अनादिमायया सुप्तो यदा जीवः प्रबुध्यते । अजमनिद्रमस्वप्नमद्वैतं बुध्यते तदा ॥

*anādimāyayā supto yadā jīvaḥ prabudhyate | ajam anidram asvapnam advaitaṃ budhyate tadā ||*

When the individual Soul, asleep owing to the beginning-less Māyā, is awakened, he then realizes the unborn, sleepless, dreamless non-duality.

**Commentary**: I.16 is the soteriological punchline of the *Āgama Prakaraṇa*. The *jīva* (individual soul) has been *supta* — asleep — not in the ordinary sense but in the sense of the great sleep of Māyā-induced non-recognition. This sleep is *anādi* — beginning-less — which means it has no original moment of falling asleep; there is no point at which ignorance began. And what is recognized when the awakening occurs? Not a new state but the *ajam* (unborn), *anidram* (sleepless), *asvapnam* (dreamless), *advaitam* (non-dual) — Turīya, which was always already the case.

### Cross-Tradition Resonance
- Pratyabhijñāhṛdayam 6: *tanmayo māyāpramātā* — the ordinary self consists entirely of contracted mind; Prājña is exactly this: the causal body that is Māyā at its most latent, just before the full contraction into individual experiencer
- Plotinus, *Enneads* V.1.12: the One as self-sufficient, needing nothing, containing all potentialities in undifferentiated unity — a Western structural parallel to *prajñānaghana*
- Buddhist *samāpatti* states, particularly *nirodha-samāpatti* (cessation of perception and feeling): a contemplatively induced analog to deep sleep that Gauḍapāda likely has in mind when distinguishing Prājña from Turīya

### Practice (*Abhyāsa*)
In the moment of waking from deep sleep, before the waking mind has fully consolidated, there is a brief window where something is present that belongs to neither sleep nor waking — a bare, contentless awareness. This is the closest most people come to sensing Turīya experientially. Cultivate the habit of pausing in this threshold moment. Not to grasp it — that immediately collapses it into the waking state — but to allow a recognition that was not absent during sleep.

