## Gauḍapāda's Kārikā: Extended Philosophical Commentary

### *Āgama Prakaraṇa* — Tradition · 29 Verses

The first *prakaraṇa* closely follows the Upaniṣad's analysis. Its distinctive contributions are:

**The causality diagnosis** (I.11–12): establishing that the first three quarters are conditioned (by cause and effect, or cause alone) while Turīya alone is uncaused — not one term in the causal chain but the ground in which causality appears.

**The Māyā identification** (I.16): naming the great sleep as *anādimāyā* — beginning-less Māyā — which means bondage has no original moment of onset and therefore liberation is not a new achievement but a recognition of what was never lost.

**The nine creation theories** (I.6–9): systematically surveying all theories of how the universe originated (*Prāṇavādins, Puruṣavādins, Svapnamāyāvādins, Icchāvādins, Kālavādins, Bhogārtham-vādins, Krīḍārtham-vādins, Devasvabhāvavādins* and others). Gauḍapāda's strategy is not to refute each but to show that the very premise — that there *was* an origination — is what *ajātivāda* challenges. By mapping all creation theories, he reveals their common assumption as the problem.

---

### *Vaitathya Prakaraṇa* — Unreality · 38 Verses

The second *prakaraṇa* is Gauḍapāda's sustained argument for the equivalence of waking and dream:

**The epistemological parity argument** (II.1–8): Dreams seem unreal from the waking standpoint. But the basis for this judgment is spatial (dream objects are enclosed within the body) and temporal (dream experiences are too brief to be real journeys). Neither criterion constitutes genuine ontological superiority for the waking state.

**The structural identity argument** (II.9–16): Both dream and waking have the same cognitive structure: inner imaginings seem unreal, outer apprehensions seem real. The Ātman through Māyā imagines both. *Kalpayaty ātmanātmānam ātmā devaḥ svamāyayā* (II.12) — the Self imagines itself by itself through its own Māyā.

**The key verses II.9–10** (already cited above) establish the inversion: in dream, inner = unreal, outer = real-seeming; in waking, the same. Since we know dream's "outer" was unreal, the waking's "outer" is subject to the same inquiry.

**II.32** (the pivotal verse): *na nirodho na cotpattir na baddho na ca sādhakaḥ | na mumukṣur na vai mukto ity eṣā paramārthatā ||*
*na nirodho na cotpattir na baddho na ca sādhakaḥ | na mumukṣur na vai mukto ity eṣā paramārthatā ||*

There is neither cessation nor origination, neither one in bondage nor seeker of liberation, neither one desirous of liberation nor one who is liberated — this is the highest truth.

This is the *Vaitathya*'s culminating statement: not that liberation is distant but that bondage was never real. The seeker, the path, and the liberation are all structures within the dream of duality. The "highest truth" (*paramārthatā*) is prior to all of them.

---

### *Advaita Prakaraṇa* — Non-Duality · 48 Verses

The third *prakaraṇa* is the most practice-oriented of the four. It introduces *asparśayoga* — "the yoga of non-contact" — and develops the ajāti argument in terms of consciousness itself.

**The ajāti argument proper** (III.6–9):

**III.6**: अजातस्यैव धर्मस्य जातिमिच्छन्ति वादिनः । अजातो ह्यमृतो धर्मः कथं मर्त्यतामेष्यति ॥

*ajātasyaiva dharmasya jātim icchanti vādinaḥ | ajāto hy amṛto dharmaḥ kathaṃ martyatām eṣyati ||*

The disputants wish to prove the origination of the entity which is verily unoriginated. How indeed can an unborn and therefore immortal entity pass on to mortality?

**III.7**: न भवत्यमृतं मर्त्यं न मर्त्यममृतं तथा । प्रकृतेरन्यथाभावो न कथञ्चिद्भविष्यति ॥

*na bhavatv amṛtaṃ martyaṃ na martyam amṛtaṃ tathā | prakṛter anyathābhāvo na kathañcid bhaviṣyati ||*

The immortal does not become mortal, nor likewise the mortal immortal. There would not be under any circumstances a change otherwise of [one's] nature.

**III.8**: स्वभावेनामृतो यस्य धर्मो मर्त्यतां गच्छति कृतकेनामृतस्तस्य कथं स्थास्यति निश्चलः ॥

*svabhāvenāmṛto yasya dharmo martyatāṃ gacchati | kṛtakenāmṛtas tasya kathaṃ sthāsyati niścalaḥ ||*

[He] for whom, in whose opinion, an entity immortal in [its] own nature goes to mortality — how will the immortal of his [that is, admitted by him] artificially made [or, subject to artificial effort] remain changeless [or, unmoving]?

**Commentary**: The argument has elegant logical force. If consciousness is inherently unborn and immortal (as the tradition claims), then the question "how did it become bound?" is malformed. An unborn thing cannot *become* mortal. If it appears to, the appearing-to is the problem, not a genuine transformation of nature. The cure is not to *make* consciousness free but to recognize that it never ceased to be free — the "binding" was never an ontological event but a *pratibhāsika* (apparent) overlay.

**Asparśayoga** (III.39–48): The *Advaita Prakaraṇa*'s most distinctive contribution — the yoga of non-contact:

**III.39**: असञ्जागरिते दृष्ट्वा स्वमे पश्यति तन्मयः । असत्स्वप्नेऽपि दृष्ट्वा च प्रतिबुद्धो न पश्यति ॥

*asañjāgarite dṛṣṭvā svame paśyati tanmayaḥ | asatsvapne'pi dṛṣṭvā ca pratibuddho na paśyati ||*

Having seen the unreal in the waking state, one being deeply absorbed in it sees [the same] in the dream; and having seen the unreal in the dream also, one [when] reawakened sees [it] not.

**III.44**: उपलम्भात्समाचाराद् माया हस्ती यथोच्यते । उपलम्भात्समाचारात् स्तितवस्तु तथोच्यते ॥

*upalambhāt samācārād māyāhastī yathocyate | upalambhāt samācārāt sthitavastu tathocyate ||*

As an illusion-elephant is spoken of [as real] because of perception [and] of the prevailing etiquette, similarly 'things exist' is spoken of [as depicting a real state of things] because of perception [and] of the prevailing etiquette.

**III.45**: नास्वाद्येत्सुखं तत्र निःसंगः प्रज्ञया भवेत् । निश्चलं निश्चरिच्चित्तमेकीकुर्यात्प्रयत्नतः ॥

*nāsvādyet sukhaṃ tatra niḥsaṃgaḥ prajñayā bhavet | niścalaṃ niścaric cittam ekīkuryāt prayatnataḥ ||*

One should not relish pleasure there [in Samādhi]; one should be free from attachment through discernment; one should unify, by effort, the steadied mind [if it be] moving out [towards objects of enjoyment].

**III.46**: यदा न लीयते चित्तं न च विक्षिप्यते पुनः । अनिङ्गनमनाभासं निष्पन्नं ब्रह्म तत्तदा ॥

*yadā na līyate cittaṃ na ca vikṣipyate punaḥ | aniṅganam anābhāsaṃ niṣpannaṃ brahma tat tadā ||*

When the mind does not lie low, and is not again tossed about, then that — being without movement and not presenting any appearance — culminates into Brahman.

**Commentary on III.44–46**: *Asparśayoga* — the yoga of non-contact — is Gauḍapāda's name for the meditative path that corresponds to *ajātivāda*. *Asparśa* (without touch, without contact) does not mean withdrawal from experience but a specific quality of relationship to it: consciousness engages with appearances without being "contaminated" (*lipta*) by them, because contamination requires believing the appearances to be independently real. III.44 is key: the illusory elephant is spoken of as real because of two social-epistemic facts — *upalambha* (perception, it is perceived) and *samācāra* (convention, it is spoken of conventionally as real). These are the two pillars of *vyāvahārika* (conventional) reality. Gauḍapāda does not deny either. He simply notes that conventional reality is not the same as *pāramārthika* (ultimate) reality.

III.46 describes the meditative state: the mind neither absorbed in an object (*na liyate* — not lying low) nor scattered (*na vikṣipyate*) — not suppressed and not agitated. This is the precise middle ground the Vijñāna Bhairava Tantra maps through 112 different entry points. Gauḍapāda describes the goal; the VBT provides the methodology.

---

### *Alātaśānti Prakaraṇa* — Quenching the Firebrand · 100 Verses

The fourth *prakaraṇa* is the most philosophically radical in Sanskrit literature. Its name comes from the central image of verses 47–52: a firebrand (*alāta*) whirled in the dark appears to create circles of fire — straight lines of apparent causality, circles of apparent continuity — but when the firebrand stops, the appearances vanish without residue. This is *ajātivāda* in image form.

**The salutation controversy** (IV.1): Gauḍapāda salutes the one who realized by *jñāna* almost like the sky that the *dharmas* (entities) are not different from the knowable — and calls this one *dvipadāṃ varam* (the best of bipeds). Karmarkar reads this as a salutation to a realized Vedāntic teacher. Bhattacharya reads it as a salutation to the Buddha. The controversy reflects the *Alātaśānti*'s sustained engagement with Buddhist philosophy — it reads Buddhist texts, uses Buddhist terminology, and then argues that Buddhism fell short of the final recognition. The term *ādibuddha* in IV.92 (enlightened from the very beginning) is either Gauḍapāda's creative appropriation of Buddhist vocabulary for Vedāntic ends, or evidence of deep Buddhist influence.

**The firebrand sequence** (IV.47–52): Read from the Sanskrit we have examined:

**IV.49**: अलाते स्पन्दमाने वै नाभासा अन्यतोभुवः । न तथोऽन्यत्र निस्पन्दाद्भालातं प्रविशन्ति ते ॥

*alāte spandamāne vai nābhāsā anyatobhuvaḥ | na tato'nyatra nispandāc chālātaṃ praviśanti te ||*

When the fire-brand is verily shaking up, the appearances do not arise from anything else; as a result of non-shaking up, [the appearances are] not elsewhere other than there, nor do they enter the fire-brand.

**IV.50**: न निर्गता अलाताच्चे द्रव्यत्वाभावयोगतः । विज्ञानेऽपि तथैव स्युराभासस्याविशेषतः ॥

*na nirgatā alātāc ce dravyatvābhāvayogataḥ | vijñāne'pi tathaiva syur ābhāsasyāviśeṣataḥ ||*

They do not get out from the fire-brand, owing to their connection with the absence of the nature of a substance; they would be just like that in respect of the vijñāna also, on account of the non-difference in appearance.

**IV.51**: विज्ञाने स्पन्दमाने वै नाभासा अन्यतोभुवः । न तथोऽन्यत्र निस्पन्दान्न विज्ञानं विशन्ति ते ॥

*vijñāne spandamāne vai nābhāsā anyatobhuvaḥ | na tato'nyatra nispandān na vijñānaṃ viśanti te ||*

When the vijñāna is verily vibrating, the appearances do not arise from anything else; as a result of non-vibration, they [the appearances are] not elsewhere other than there, nor do they enter the vijñāna.

**IV.52**: न निर्गतास्ते विज्ञानाद्द्रव्यत्वाभावयोगतः । कार्यकारणताभावाद्घतोऽचिन्त्याः सदैव ते ॥

*na nirgatās te vijñānād dravyatvābhāvayogataḥ | kāryakāraṇatābhāvād ghato'cintyāḥ sadaiva te ||*

They do not get out from the vijñāna, owing to their connection with the absence of the nature of a substance; because of the absence of the relation of cause and effect, they are ever and anon incomprehensible.

**Commentary on IV.49–52**: The firebrand argument is Gauḍapāda's most elegant and the one that most directly addresses Buddhist *vijñānavāda* (consciousness-only philosophy). The Vijñānavādins (Yogācāra Buddhists) held that all apparent objects are only appearances in consciousness — there are no external material objects, only vijñāna (consciousness) and its self-generated appearances. Gauḍapāda agrees with the *first* part (the appearances are not from anywhere else, not from external matter) but argues against the *second* part: if appearances are not independent substances (*dravyatvābhāvayoga*), then they do not enter or leave consciousness as if crossing a threshold. The relation is more intimate — they are the vibration (*spandita*) of consciousness itself. When the firebrand stops moving, the circles vanish — not because they went somewhere but because they were never independently there to begin with. Vijñāna similarly: when the *spanda* (vibration) of mind ceases, appearances do not travel to another location. They were always *cittaspandita* — mind-vibration — and mind-vibration has no independent substance to travel. Therefore: *kāryakāraṇatābhāvāt* — in the absence of the relation of cause and effect — they are *acintyāḥ* — always incomprehensible. *Ajātivāda* is not a negative statement but an ontological one: what appears as causally generated was never in the causal order to begin with.

**The Buddhist dialogue** (IV.73–100): Gauḍapāda systematically surveys Buddhist positions:

**IV.73**: योऽस्ति कल्पितसंवृत्या परमार्थेन नास्त्यसौ । परतन्त्राभिसंवृत्या स्याच्चास्ति परमार्थतः ॥

*yo'sti kalpitasaṃvṛtyā paramārthena nāsty asau | paratantrābhisaṃvṛtyā syāc cāsti paramārthataḥ ||*

What is on account of the imagined phenomenal experience, that exists not in reality. It may exist in accordance with the phenomenal experience accepted in other schools [of philosophy], [but it] exists not in reality.

**IV.83**: अस्ति नास्त्यस्ति नास्तीति नास्ति नास्तीति वा पुनः । चलस्थिरोभयाभावैराचृणोत्येव बालिशः ॥

*asti nāsty asti nāstīti nāsti nāstīti vā punaḥ | calasthirobhayābhāvair ācṛṇoty eva bāliśaḥ ||*

Is, is not, is [and] is not, is not is not — thus again the untrained one (*bāliśa*) does encompass [the Bhagavat] with [notions of him as] moving, steady, both [moving and steady] and free from both.

**Commentary on IV.83**: This is Gauḍapāda's adoption of Nāgārjuna's *catuṣkoṭi* (fourfold negation): is / is not / both / neither. The untrained (*bāliśa*) mind tries to locate reality within one of these four ontological positions. Gauḍapāda — following the Māṇḍūkya's apophatic method — refuses all four. Reality (*paramārtha*) is not locatable within the framework of *asti/nāsti* (existence/non-existence). But where Nāgārjuna uses this to point to *śūnyatā* (emptiness), Gauḍapāda uses it to point to *ātmā* — the Self that is prior to both the affirmation and the negation.

**IV.92–93** (already cited): The *ādibuddha* verses — all entities are enlightened from the very beginning. This is the *Alātaśānti*'s culminating positive statement and its most explicit convergence with and divergence from Buddhist *tathāgatagarbha* (Buddha-nature) doctrine. The convergence: all beings carry the luminous nature from the beginning. The divergence: Gauḍapāda identifies this with *ātmā* as Brahman; the Buddhist tradition is careful not to identify it with any substantial self.

